17592186045417] [17592186192983 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Po(θ)$ with unknown rate parameter $θ$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_{1} = 3, x_{2} = 4, x_{3} = 2$.\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n$l(θ) = aθ + b\\ln θ - \\ln c$.\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b44bc46-2ed5-5c39-aeeb-2a4f2782bbaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192983 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193506 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193506 #edb/ref 1061 38134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193506 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5b9252a-b7f5-52fe-a10d-b66f5fe665fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193506 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193682 #edb/ref 1061 42269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13d18ec0-09d2-5510-8edc-00b0f1781b94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193683 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193683 #edb/ref 1061 42275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193683 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "012bca18-0bfe-527e-a3ee-dcfee75eafd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193683 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193684 #edb/ref 1061 42276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f297c08f-8ed0-5ea9-a51f-0fdf821d69d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193691 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193691 #edb/ref 1061 42317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46ba308c-f318-5b7c-a01b-76e427c7a010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193898 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n$ is \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6\n$$\n\nis an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is the unknown probability of success, then the likelihood function of the sample \n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{4}(1-\\theta)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function contains lots of products, which can be cumbersome to work with. To make things easier, we convert the products into sums by finding the natural logarithm of $L(\\theta),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(L(\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta )\\cdot f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta )⋯f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta ))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta ))+⋯+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new function $l(\\theta)$ is called the **log-likelihood function** of the sample.\n\nFor our Bernoulli example above, the corresponding log-likelihood function is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193898 #edb/ref 1065 7002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193898 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0100980e-b08d-530a-9a07-668209e0af44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193898 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193901 #edb/ref 1064 "The graph of the likelihood function \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^4(1\\!-\\!\\theta)^2\n$$\n\nfor the Bernoulli distribution we saw earlier is shown below. \n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nThe graph of the corresponding log-likelihood function\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 4 \\ln \\theta + 2 \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7f0ba3147504869e162dd371f98d5d6c492e89345f80c75037bdc82a6006d1c.png)\n\nAlthough the graphs look quite different, they both have a maximum at the same point $\\theta = \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}.$ This will become important later. \n\nLet's see more examples of finding the log-likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193901 #edb/ref 1065 7015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193901 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7fa77114-72fa-5d0d-89f5-c85ec7793285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193901 #edb/ref 1067 "Likelihood vs Log-Likelihood" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
